.\" karvi-stream(1): the manual page of karvi's stream word. Written by hand except
.\" the regions between the markers, which tools/mangen rewrites from the
.\" word's help text (make generate).
.TH KARVI-STREAM 1 "" "karvi"
.\" Paths and options are not hyphenated, and lines are not stretched.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
karvi\-stream \- read a run line by line from standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED SYNOPSIS: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
\fBkarvi\fR \fBstream\fR
.br
\fBkarvi\fR \fB\-\fR
.\" END GENERATED SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" BEGIN GENERATED DESCRIPTION: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
.PP
Reads a run line by line from standard input and executes it as run would.
A line is skipped when blank or when its first character is ! or #. A line
beginning with a dash is one run option, one dash or two alike: the word,
then its value as the rest of the line after a space or =, so \-\-target
router1, \-target=router1, \-\-tl router1 router2, \-\-dispatch parallel, \-\-dp.
A value wholly wrapped in one pair of quotes loses them, as a shell would
remove them: \-\-cmd "show clock". A line leaving text no option takes is
dropped. Any other line is one command, sent as written, quotes and all; \er
at its end is read as \-\-cmd reads it, and a line of \er alone sends a blank
line. The targets and options stay from one job to the next, and so do the
commands given in option form (\-\-cmd, \-c, \-\-command, \-\-cf); a command given
as a bare line is the job's alone. \-\-expect, \-\-blind, \-\-blind\-return,
\-\-timeout, and \-\-maxbytes lines attach to the command before them. \-\-cf,
\-\-tf, and \-\-tfr may not name \-: standard input is the stream.
.SS "Directives, each a whole line, one dash or two"
.TP
\fB\-\-go\fR, \fB\-\-sendit\fR
Execute the draft as run; the bare\-line
commands clear and everything else stays;
with no command to send, a notice
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\fR
Empty the bare\-line commands; the targets,
options, and option\-form commands stay
.TP
\fB\-\-purge\-commands\fR
Empty every command (from \-\-purge\-c)
.TP
\fB\-\-purge\-targets\fR
Remove every target input (from \-\-purge\-t):
\-\-target, \-\-tl, \-\-tf, \-\-tfr, \-\-site,
\-\-device\-group, \-\-all, \-\-select\-platform
.TP
\fB\-\-reset\fR
Empty the draft
.TP
\fB\-\-restart\fR
Empty the draft and read the configuration
again, as the stream's start does; a
reading that fails ends the stream
.TP
\fB\-\-end\fR, \fB\-\-quit\fR, \fB\-\-exit\fR
Leave without executing; so do EOF (Ctrl\-D)
and Ctrl\-C
.PP
Typed at a terminal, a line is edited with Ctrl\-A, Ctrl\-E, Ctrl\-K, Ctrl\-U,
Ctrl\-W, and the arrows, and the up arrow recalls earlier lines. A line the
parser refuses is reported with its number and dropped. The exit is the
last executed job's, 0 when none ran; a read failure or a line over 1 MiB
ends the stream with stream_input_read_failed, and a \-\-restart whose
reading fails ends it with the reading's code. A restart keeps the line
count, the last exit, and the recalled lines. Global options go before
the word: karvi \-\-quiet \-\-config FILE stream.
.SS "Options"
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-h\fR
Show this help
.\" END GENERATED DESCRIPTION
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B printf "\-\-site nyc\enshow clock\en\-\-go\en" | karvi stream
One job, the site's devices and one command.
.TP
.B karvi \-
Lines typed at the terminal: targets and options stay from one job to
the next, and
.B \-\-go
sends the commands typed since the last one.
.TP
.B karvi \-\-quiet stream <change.txt
A file of runs, global options before the word.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR karvi (1),
.BR karvi\-run (1)
